WEBVTT

00:24:27.109 --> 00:24:30.323
and was like, "This novelist
is not going to be good enough for me.

00:24:30.347 --> 00:24:31.843
I'm going to use Ann Patchett."

00:24:31.867 --> 00:24:36.585
And it was the most exciting example
I've ever seen of how ideas live,

00:24:36.609 --> 00:24:39.416
because there's no explanation for that.

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:43.446
That is beyond any rational
explanation whatsoever.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:46.034
And that book, of course,
became "State of Wonder,"

00:24:46.058 --> 00:24:47.876
which is an extraordinary novel

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:51.388
that she then made her own,
obviously, and is amazing.

00:24:51.412 --> 00:24:54.486
I'm so glad I didn't have to write it,
because she did it better.

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:55.540
(Laughter)

00:24:55.564 --> 00:24:59.277
CA: So I have to say, this is
an incredible story, obviously.

00:24:59.301 --> 00:25:02.294
But the skeptical part of me is going,